class CreateWorkflowRequestRetryPolicy(TypedDict, total=False):
    """Retry behaviour for the WORKFLOW RUN itself — not the connector-call retry policy on connectorManifest[].retryPolicy, which is a different shape (initialDelayMs/backoffMultiplier/maxDelayMs) and IS fully honoured. `maxAttempts` is total attempts INCLUDING the first, and defaults to 1 — so retry is opt-in and a workflow that declares no policy runs exactly once. On `standard`/`long` it bounds Cloud Run task retries (`shouldRetryBatchTask`), which fire for a retryable failure: a transient SystemError (a platform fault, or a dropped or timed-out database connection), a timeout, a 5xx the workflow RETURNED, or a connector 5xx/408/425/429 that escaped user code. A deliberate `throw` in user code, code that does not compile, a 4xx, an out-of-memory and a cancellation are deterministic and never retry. Attempts RESUME rather than re-run: the executionId is unchanged, so `ctx.isRetry` + `fastn.checkpoint` continue where the last attempt stopped. `initialIntervalMs` / `backoffCoefficient` / `maximumIntervalMs` are honoured as an in-task sleep before a re-run, which is billable and comes out of the run's own timeout. On `instant` it bounds an in-process re-run loop that retries the same failures as the async tiers, and only when the run is not tier-escalation-eligible (escalateOnTimeout forces a single attempt). The loop discards the previous attempt rather than resuming it, honours the interval fields between attempts, and stops early when the next attempt cannot fit the tier's ~25s gateway budget — so a connector failure whose own retries already spent that budget gets no further attempt. Same declared number, resume semantics on one tier and re-run semantics on the other. See docs/services/WORKFLOWS.md."""
    maxAttempts: int
    initialIntervalMs: int
    maximumIntervalMs: int
    backoffCoefficient: float


